ewlabel{fig:regime_map}{{1}{2}{Detection phase diagram (Pythia-410M, fp32, PermG direction-checked). Each cell shows the number of FDR-significant canary$	o $diversity pairs (of~8) at a given contamination strength~$\alpha $ and observation window~$T$. The dashed curve is the fitted detection boundary $\alpha _c(T) \propto T^{-\gamma }$ with $\gamma \in [0.64, 2.08]$ (profile 95\% CI; \S ef {sec:theory}). Diamond markers ($\diamond $) flag a non-monotonic anomaly at $\alpha {=}0.60$ (0/8 at $T{\leq }20$); the star ($\star $) marks its dissolution at $T{=}25$ (5/8)}{figure.caption.1}{}}

ewlabel{fig:metric_trajectory}{{2}{7}{Metric trajectories at $\alpha {=}1.0$ (Pythia-410M, 4~seeds). Token entropy drops at generation~1; distinct-1 declines from generations~2--3, with a consistent one-generation canary lead across all four seeds (shaded: inter-seed range). This pattern corresponds to 24/24 forward and 0/24 reverse FDR-significant canary$	o $diversity pairs (6 per seed, mauve excluded)}{figure.caption.11}{}}

ewlabel{fig:dose_response}{{3}{7}{PermG dose-response profile (Pythia-410M, $T{=}10$, fp32). Sigmoid fit (empirical trend; shaded 95\% CI) yields $\alpha _c{=}0.483$. Three confirmed regimes: blind ($\alpha {\leq }0.45$, 0/8), onset ($\alpha {\approx }0.50$, 6/8), detection ($\alpha {\geq }0.75$); non-monotonic anomaly at $\alpha {=}0.60$ (0/8) (out of 8 canary$	o $downstream pairs). The sigmoid is a descriptive fit to observed detection rates, not a predictive model}{figure.caption.12}{}}

ewlabel{fig:method_comparison}{{4}{7}{Method sensitivity: FDR-significant forward pairs (\%) across contamination levels (Pythia-410M, seed~42, fp32). PermG (dark blue) achieves 0\% null-control FPR with stable 75\% detection at $\alpha \geq 0.50$. Dashed line: 5\% nominal. Full table in Appendix~ef {app:dose_response}}{figure.caption.13}{}}

ewlabel{fig:intervention}{{5}{8}{Canary-triggered intervention (Pythia-410M, $\alpha {=}1.0$). Predictive cessation on entropy (generation~1) reduces peak perplexity by 64\% versus reactive triggering on distinct-1 (generation~2)}{figure.caption.15}{}}

ewlabel{fig:window_sensitivity}{{6}{12}{Consensus stability across minimum observation windows $T_{\min } \in \{5, \ldots , 10\}$. Left: $K{\geq }3$ consensus count at $\alpha {=}0.75$ (3 seeds). Right: per-method detection count across $T_{\min }$. Consensus stabilises at $T_{\min }{=}5$, confirming that the 10-generation default provides ample statistical power}{figure.caption.31}{}}

ewlabel{fig:spec_curve}{{7}{13}{Specification curve analysis (1{,}160 configurations). Each point is one analytical specification (method $	imes $ pair $	imes $ seed); lines trace detection rates across contamination levels. Bottom bar indicates the method for each specification. The blind$	o $onset jump at $\alpha _c{\approx }0.50$ is robust across specifications}{figure.caption.39}{}}

ewlabel{fig:cusum_monitoring}{{8}{15}{CUSUM sequential monitoring of token entropy at two contamination levels ($\alpha {=}0.50$, $\alpha {=}0.75$; seed~42). Null control limits calibrated from $\alpha {=}0.0$ runs. At both operating points, the simple $2\sigma $ threshold triggers at the same generation as CUSUM ($k{=}0.25$), confirming that the large initial shift dominates any cumulative memory advantage}{figure.caption.52}{}}

ewlabel{fig:14b_phase}{{9}{18}{Pythia-1.4B phase diagram (PermG forward, 6-pair basis). Each cell shows per-seed detection fractions (s42\,|\,s44 for $T{=}10$; s42\,|\,s43 for $T{=}15$). The detection boundary ($\alpha _c \approx 0.50$) is qualitatively consistent with the 410M results, confirming cross-scale generality}{figure.caption.68}{}}

ewlabel{fig:per_method_phase}{{10}{19}{Per-method detection heatmaps (Pythia-410M, seed~42, fp32, $T \in \{10, 20\}$). Each panel shows forward-direction detection rate (\%) across contamination levels for one method. PermG exhibits the sharpest onset (0\%$	o $75\% between $\alpha {=}0.45$ and 0.50) with zero null-control detection, while Naive XCorr saturates across all levels including null. TY and Threshold show diffuse, unstable profiles}{figure.caption.71}{}}

ewlabel{fig:permg_power}{{11}{20}{PermG detection power as a function of coupling strength $ eta $ for five observation lengths ($T{=}6$--$20$; 100 replications $	imes $ 5{,}000 permutations, BH FDR $q{=}0.05$). Power increases with both $ eta $ and $T$; at $T{=}6$--$8$, PermG remains underpowered ($<$20\%) even at $ eta {=}2.0$, consistent with the $T{=}10$ minimum required for reliable detection in our experimental setting}{figure.caption.77}{}}

ewlabel{fig:ar1_diagnostic}{{12}{21}{Empirical AR(1) coefficients by contamination level (Pythia-410M, seed~42, $T{=}10$). (a)~Token entropy: raw $\varphi $ transitions from anti-correlated ($-0.51$) at $\alpha {=}0$ to near-unity ($1.00$) at $\alpha {=}1.0$; first-differencing reduces all values below the $\varphi {=}0.95$ FPR danger zone (dashed red). (b)~Distinct-1: high raw $\varphi $ ($0.81$--$0.97$) across all $\alpha $ including null reflects deterministic trend, not stochastic AR persistence; first-differenced $\varphi $ drops to near zero}{figure.caption.83}{}}

ewlabel{fig:shapley}{{13}{22}{Shapley value decomposition of each method's marginal contribution to FPR (positive bars, red) and FNR (negative bars, green/blue), computed from 6 null seeds (36 pairs) and 45 signal experiments (270 pairs). DiffXCorr achieves the best combined Shapley ($-0.299$) via near-zero FPR; PermG ranks second ($-0.141$) as the best directional method. Annotations indicate whether each method's contribution increases or reduces overall error}{figure.caption.86}{}}

ewlabel{fig:null_fpr_diagnostic}{{14}{23}{Null-control FPR by method ($\alpha {=}0.0$, 6~seeds, 6-pair basis). Bars: pooled FPR (rejections/36); dots: per-seed FPR. Naive XCorr (91.7\%) and Toda--Yamamoto (38.9\%) vastly exceed the 5\% nominal level (dashed). PermG (11.1\%) is the lowest-FPR directional method; DiffXCorr (2.8\%) is lowest overall but lacks forward/reverse separation}{figure.caption.90}{}}

ewlabel{fig:bootstrap_fpr}{{15}{23}{Bootstrap distribution of PermG FPR (10{,}000 resamples, 6 null seeds). Mean FPR $= 11.1\%$ (red line); bootstrap percentile 95\% CI $[0.0\%, 22.2\%]$ (orange band). The Clopper--Pearson cluster-adjusted CI reported in the text ($[1.2\%, 30.4\%]$) is wider due to ICC correction. The distribution is right-skewed: four of six seeds show zero false positives}{figure.caption.92}{}}

ewlabel{fig:detection_surface}{{16}{24}{Detection rate surface over the $\alpha 	imes T$ operating space (PermG forward, 3 seeds). Contour lines indicate 25\%, 50\%, and 75\% detection rates. The fitted power-law boundary $\alpha _c(T)$ (dashed) separates the blind zone ($\alpha \leq 0.45$) from the reliable detection regime ($\alpha \geq 0.75$). The transition zone ($0.50 \leq \alpha \leq 0.60$) widens at shorter $T$}{figure.caption.97}{}}

ewlabel{fig:channel_effect}{{17}{25}{Channel effect size asymmetry. (a)~First-differencing retains comparable correlation magnitude for diversity ($95.2\%$) and perplexity ($100.2\%$) channels, ruling out blanket power loss. (b)~Despite equivalent $|r|$ retention, Granger $F$-statistic is ${\sim }6	imes $ higher for diversity ($F{=}67.8$) than perplexity ($F{=}11.5$), reflecting a genuine temporal structure difference: diversity channels exhibit canary-leading lags while perplexity co-moves contemporaneously}{figure.caption.105}{}}

ewlabel{fig:cascade_onset}{{18}{26}{Cascade onset timeline (Pythia-410M, WikiText, fp32, 5\% threshold). Token entropy and ECE reach onset at generation~1 (Phase~1, blue shading), while distinct-$n$ metrics follow at generation~2 (Phase~2, orange shading). Perplexity onset occurs at generation~4 only at $\alpha {\geq }0.50$; MAUVE onset is condition-dependent. The ordering $t_H \leq t_D$ holds across all 5 conditions (2 seeds $	imes $ 3 $\alpha $ levels minus $\alpha {=}0.25$ s42, which has identical Phase~1 and~2 onset)}{figure.caption.108}{}}

ewlabel{fig:cross_arch_heatmap}{{19}{31}{Cross-architecture PermG forward detection rates at $\alpha {=}1.0$ across 15 model-seed combinations spanning five families (Pythia, GPT-2, OPT, Qwen, SmolLM3; 345M--6.9B). Dark bars: PermG forward detection; light bars: $K{\geq }3$ multi-method consensus. C4-domain training achieves 8/8; SmolLM3-3B (3/8) and Pythia-1.4B s42 (2/8) are the two below-threshold conditions}{figure.caption.129}{}}

